! `repeat-word`: a connective, then the previous word again. `[COPY1]` is the
! word the host bound to its first register, and the dictionary never sees it,
! which is what keeps the automaton finite.
!
! `scripts/check-hold.py` replays every case through a real Plover engine and
! compares, because a bound word is the other thing Plover's dictionary API has
! no way to pass to a lookup.

!buffer
KAT
^SKP
!buffer
HAOER
^OR
!buffer
KAT
^-T
!buffer
KAT
^*F

! The register is bound before the record writes, so the echo is the word the
! stroke found and not the one it just wrote.
!buffer
KAT
^SKP
^SKP

! A unit that wrote two words leaves the newest as the register.
!buffer
-FT
^SKP

! Undo takes the connective and its echo back together, because they are one
! translation.
!buffer
KAT
^SKP
!undo
^OR
